In December 1938, Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer, a young museum curator in the town of East London, South Africa, checked a fishing boat's catch. Among the sharks and fish she saw a large, steel-blue fish with fleshy fins that looked almost like stubby legs. She sketched it and sent the drawing to a fish expert, J. L. B. Smith.

Smith recognised it as a coelacanth, a fish known from fossils but thought to have died out about 66 million years ago. He named it Latimeria after her. In the late 1990s, a second [[species|species]] was found in Indonesia, when a scientist on honeymoon spotted one in a fish market.

{{story:sharks-old}}

Many animals with horns today, such as antelope and wild sheep, use them mainly on each other, to win mates or space. In 2009, scientists studied dozens of Triceratops skulls and counted the healed injuries. Most were on the sides of the frill and on the cheekbones.

That is where a rival's horns would hit if two animals locked heads and twisted. A cousin called Centrosaurus, with one big nose horn and tiny brow horns, had far fewer injuries there. The wounds had healed, so these animals lived through their fights.

{{story:rhino-kaziranga}}

The Zhangye Danxia landform in Gansu province, China, is one of the most famous. Its layers of red sandstone and siltstone were laid down in an ancient basin many millions of years ago. Then the collision of India with Asia, the same crash that raised the [[@himalaya-form|Himalayas]], squeezed and lifted them.

Today they look like waves of red, orange, yellow and white. In 2019, Zhangye became a UNESCO Global Geopark, a place protected for its rocks and landscapes. Boardwalks and viewing platforms let visitors watch the colours glow at sunset.

{{story:rust}}

A big tree sucks water up from the soil and breathes it out through its leaves as vapour. In the Amazon, scientists estimate the trees release billions of tonnes of water into the air every day. The water forms clouds that drift inland and fall as rain far away. People call these "flying rivers".

Without trees, many regions would get less rain. Forests in India's Western Ghats also help feed the rivers that flow east across the Deccan. Take away the trees and the land dries, and in a dry land, fewer trees can grow back.

{{story:easter-island}}

The first Mars rover, Sojourner, landed in 1997. It was about the size of a microwave oven. In 2004, two solar-powered rovers arrived, named Spirit and Opportunity. Opportunity was meant to work for 90 days. It kept going for almost 15 years, until a giant dust storm covered its [[@solar-panels|solar panels]] in 2018.

Curiosity landed in 2012 and has spent years slowly climbing a mountain inside Gale Crater. China landed its own rover, Zhurong, in 2021. Each mission taught engineers something new about [[@robots|robots]] that must work far from any mechanic.

{{story:robots}}

People hunted wild horses for meat long before they rode them. Around 5,500 years ago, people at Botai in Kazakhstan were keeping horses and may have milked them. But DNA studies show those animals were not the ancestors of today's horses.

A big study published in 2021 traced modern domestic horses to the grasslands of southern Russia about 4,200 years ago. Within a few centuries, they spread across Asia and Europe. Light chariots with spoked [[@wheel-invented|wheels]] appeared around the same time, giving armies a terrifying new weapon.

{{story:wheel-invented}}

Polymers aren't only made in factories. [[@silk|Silk]] is a polymer spun by silkworms. [[@clothes-cotton|Cotton]] and wood are made of [[cellulose|cellulose]], a polymer of sugar. Natural rubber comes from the sap of rubber trees, which grow in Kerala. Even [[@dna|DNA]], the instructions in your cells, is a polymer.

The first plastics borrowed from nature. In 1862, the British inventor Alexander Parkes showed Parkesine, made from plant cellulose. In 1907, the Belgian-American chemist Leo Baekeland made Bakelite from chemicals alone. It was hard, heat-resistant and didn't conduct electricity. It replaced shellac, a resin produced by lac insects that was harvested in India and used for electrical insulation and gramophone records.

{{story:dna}}

A tyre's tread has a few parts. Long grooves running around the tyre carry water away. Cross grooves link them to the sides. Between the grooves sit blocks of rubber that grip the road.

Many tyres also have sipes, tiny slits cut into the blocks. When the tyre flexes, the sipes open slightly and grab at wet or icy surfaces. Winter tyres in snowy places like Canada or Himachal can have hundreds of them. Off-road tyres have big, chunky blocks with wide gaps that dig into mud and then fling it out.

{{story:formula-one}}

The roots of jazz reach back to slavery in America. Enslaved Africans brought rhythms, songs and ways of making music from many parts of West and Central Africa. They were often forbidden to keep their traditions, but music survived in work songs, church songs and gatherings.

In New Orleans, enslaved and free Black people gathered on Sundays at a place called Congo Square to drum and dance, in the late 1700s and early 1800s. Decades later, after slavery ended, the blues gave voice to hard lives, and ragtime filled dance halls with lively piano music.

{{story:slavery-freedom}}

Ravi Varma became famous for portraits of royal families. His real fame, though, came from scenes of old stories: Shakuntala writing a love letter on a [[@lotus|lotus]] leaf, Damayanti talking to a swan, Arjuna and Subhadra. One of his paintings won a prize at an international exhibition in Vienna in 1873.

He travelled across India looking at clothes, jewellery and faces to make his pictures feel real. He often dressed his women in a sari draped in a style common in Maharashtra, and some historians think his pictures helped make that drape popular across the country.

{{story:printing-press}}

By the 1500s, people in Germany were bringing fir trees into their homes and decorating them with apples and sweets. Evergreen trees, which stay green through winter, had long been symbols of life. In 1848 a London newspaper printed a picture of Queen Victoria's family around a decorated tree, and the custom spread quickly in Britain and America.

In 1843, Henry Cole had the first Christmas cards printed in London. Carols, songs sung at Christmas, are much older. Many popular ones, such as "Silent Night", first sung in Austria in 1818, are still sung around the world in hundreds of languages.

{{story:calendars}}

Cristiano Ronaldo was born in 1985 in Funchal, on the Portuguese island of Madeira. He left home at 12 to join Sporting Lisbon's academy. At 18 he moved to Manchester United in England.

Ronaldo is famous for his training. He works on strength, speed and jumping, and keeps a strict diet. He has won the Champions League five times, with Manchester United and Real Madrid, and is its all-time top scorer. He is also the top scorer in men's international football, with more than 130 goals for Portugal by 2024.

{{story:hormones}}

People have built stadiums for thousands of years. The ancient Greeks raced in stadiums at Olympia. In Rome, the [[@colosseum|Colosseum]] opened in 80 CE and held perhaps 50,000 people. It had numbered entrances so crowds could find their seats quickly, and a huge canvas awning, pulled across by sailors, to shade spectators from the sun.

Many of its ideas survive. Modern stadiums still use numbered gates, steep banks of seats and passages under the stands, called vomitories, that let crowds pour in and out.

{{story:colosseum}}

In 1947, Ambedkar became free India's first Law Minister and chairman of the Constitution's Drafting Committee. Over nearly three years, the [[@constituent-assembly|Constituent Assembly]] debated every article.

{{story:constitution}}

The finished Constitution banned untouchability. It promised equality before the law and gave every adult the vote. It was adopted on 26 November 1949, now marked as Constitution Day, and came into force on 26 January 1950. Ambedkar warned that rights on paper were only a start, and that real equality would need people to live by them.

Sun soon lost power, and for years China was split between warlords, generals with private armies. Sun's Nationalist Party, later led by Chiang Kai-shek, tried to reunite the country. The Chinese Communist Party, founded in 1921, at first worked with the Nationalists, then became their bitter enemy.

In 1934 the Communists, close to defeat, set out on the Long March, a retreat of thousands of kilometres across mountains and rivers. Most who started did not finish. But the march made Mao Zedong the party's leader.

{{story:beijing}}

In 1868 rebels from the south overthrew the shogun and "restored" the emperor. The court moved from [[@kyoto|Kyoto]] to Edo, which was renamed Tokyo, meaning "eastern capital". The emperor's reign was called *Meiji*, meaning "enlightened rule".

The new leaders moved quickly. They abolished the old feudal domains and set up a central government. They also did something hard to picture: they ended the samurai as a class. Samurai lost their payments from the state, and in 1876 most people were banned from carrying swords in public. Some samurai rebelled, and the biggest rising, in 1877, was crushed by the new army of ordinary conscripts.

{{story:bullet-trains}}

The empire reached its peak under Krishnadevaraya, who ruled from 1509 to 1529. He won wars against the Deccan sultanates and the Gajapati kings of Odisha, and he built and repaired many temples.

He also wrote poetry. His Telugu poem *Amuktamalyada* tells the story of Andal, a woman saint of Tamil Nadu. His court had eight famous poets, called the Ashtadiggajas. One of them was Tenali Ramakrishna, whose witty adventures became the [[@tenali-raman|Tenali Raman]] stories that children still love.

{{story:tenali-raman}}

The Great Pyramid of Giza in Egypt was built around 4,500 years ago as a tomb for the pharaoh Khufu. It was made of more than two million stone blocks. At about 146 m, it was the tallest building made by humans for nearly 4,000 years, until a cathedral spire in England beat it in the 1300s.

By the time Greek writers listed it, the pyramid was already older to them than the Romans are to us.

{{story:pyramids}}

Narayan wrote in English, which was unusual for an Indian storyteller in the 1930s. His first book was rejected by several publishers. A friend passed the [[manuscript|manuscript]] to the English novelist Graham Greene, who liked it and helped get it published in London. Greene and Narayan stayed friends for decades.

Narayan's later novel *The Guide*, published in 1958, won the Sahitya Akademi Award and became a famous Hindi film in 1965. He was later given the Padma Vibhushan, one of India's highest honours.

